it's summer in Australia and this  orchard would usually draw hundreds of  visitors who come to pick their own  fruit this is just one of many small  businesses devastated by the bush fires  almost all these apple trees were  destroyed by flames the trees were  uninsured and it will cost the family  about 1 million dollars to replant them  and repair the damage  my parents have owned it for in the  business for 35 years and yeah it's it  takes a bit of a toll emotionally when  you say you put so much blood sweat and  tears into producing the fruit and to  see it go up in flames it's just  devastating many farms around Australia  had already been suffering through the  relentless drought and this bushfire  season has just made their situation so  much worse but the financial impact of  this natural disaster won't just be felt  on farms it will be felt around the  country this is Australia's longest  bushfire emergency the fires began  months earlier than normal and are still  burning the most severely affected areas  only account for about 1% of the  Australian economy but the disaster is  having a global impact we'll also see a  loss of tourism coming into this country  people are already canceling holidays  and whether there's a longer term costs  associated with the image of Australia  as a burning nation analysts have  downgraded forecasts one of Australia's  biggest banks Westpac estimates it could  slash half a percent from economic  growth already there are around 1  billion dollars worth of insurance  claims and the government has committed  around one-and-a-half billion dollars to  rebuilding infrastructure but it says  there could be more money if it's needed  scientists say the bushfire season has  been exacerbated by climate change and  that's put Australia's leaders under  more pressure to raise targets to reduce  carbon emissions but coal is one of  Australia's biggest exports and prime  minister Scott Morrison has said  although policies could be evolved he  won't engage in what he called reckless  job destroying targets the government  has indicated there may be a formal  inquiry into the fires including any  role climate change may have had in  sparking one of Australia's most  expensive natural disasters ever jessica  washington al jazeera bill pin australia
 

